// Stimulator: Randall Kaufmann's high-floating stonefly and caddis dry, yellow version.

id stimulator
name "Stimulator"
type dry
imitates stonefly caddis attractor
difficulty intermediate
species trout grayling
season may june july august september october
origin originator="Randall Kaufmann" region="Oregon, USA"
description "An elk hair tail and wing, a yellow abdomen with a palmered brown hackle and wire rib, and an orange thorax with a grizzly hackle."
history """
    Randall Kaufmann's own account: in 1980 his friend Rick Verheghe did well on the
    Deschutes with a fly labelled "Hank's Special" from a bin at the Oasis Café in Maupin.
    Kaufmann refined it, David Lambroughton suggested the name in 1981, and it first
    appeared in the Kaufmann Streamborn catalogue in 1982; he says no one else was
    involved. Jim Slattery of New Jersey tells a different story (in Rusty Dunn's SWTU
    article): Kaufmann phoned him about his "Fluttering Stonefly" of the late 1970s, said
    he had slimmed the wing and shortened the front hackle, and agreed to the name
    Slattery suggested. Neither account has been independently confirmed.
    Kaufmann published it in Tying Dry Flies (1991), with the fly on the cover. He also
    says the style was not new: he mixed the Improved Sofa Pillow and the Royal Trude.
    """
fishing """
    A high-floating searching fly, easy to see even in broken pocket water, and often the
    dry in a dry-dropper rig. It imitates stoneflies of all sizes; sizes 12 to 16 also do
    for caddis, and the largest pass for grasshoppers.
    """
sources {
    "Umpqua: The Story of the Stimulator (Randall Kaufmann)" url="https://www.umpqua.com/stories/the-story-of-the-stimulator/"
    "SWTU: Jim Slattery's Original Stimulator (Rusty Dunn)" url="https://www.swtu.org/2023/03/07/jim-slatterys-original-stimulator/"
    "Fly Fisherman: A Classic American Fly: The Stimulator (Tom Rosenbauer)" url="https://www.flyfisherman.com/editorial/classic-american-fly-stimulator/455993"
    "Fly Fisherman: How to tie the Stimulator Fly (Charlie Craven)" url="https://www.flyfisherman.com/editorial/fly-tying-stimulator-fly/151967"
    "Wikipedia: Artificial fly (attractor patterns)" url="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_fly"
}
// Photos for checking proportions while authoring (npm run refcheck); never published.
references {
    "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/84/Yellow_Stimulator.jpg" page="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yellow_Stimulator.jpg" author="Mike Cline" license="CC BY-SA 4.0" note="Side view"
    "https://files.osgnetworks.tv/16/files/2013/08/Stimulator-Beauty.jpg" page="https://www.flyfisherman.com/editorial/fly-tying-stimulator-fly/151967" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Side view in the vise"
    "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/Crystal_Stimulator_Lime_Green.jpg" page="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crystal_Stimulator_Lime_Green.jpg" author="Mike Cline" license="CC BY-SA 4.0" flip=#true note="Side view, Crystal variant"
}

hook dry-3xl-curved size=10
thread orange size="6/0"

materials {
    tail elk-hair
    // Rosenbauer's dressings give fine gold wire; Craven ties copper.
    rib gold-wire
    abdomen dry-fly-dubbing color=yellow
    hackle-body cock-hackle color=brown fibre=1.0
    wing elk-hair
    hackle-thorax cock-hackle color=grizzly fibre=1.15
    thorax dry-fly-dubbing color=orange label="Fire orange dubbing"
}

steps {
    attach-thread at=0.7
    wrap-thread to=0.0
    tie-in tail length=1.0 spread=45 note="A short, flared bunch, about a gape long."
    tie-in rib
    dub abdomen to=0.62 note="Thin, with a slight taper."
    tie-in hackle-body
    wind hackle-body to=0.05 turns=6
    wind rib to=0.62 turns=6 note="Through the hackle, the other way, to protect it."
    tie-off rib
    trim hackle-body
    // Sources: the wing reaches from the front of the abdomen to the middle of the tail.
    tie-in wing length=2.4 angle=14 spread=85 note="About twice as much hair as the tail, flaring over the top half of the shank."
    trim wing
    tie-in hackle-thorax
    dub thorax to=0.92 note="Thin, to just short of the eye."
    wind hackle-thorax to=0.92 turns=4
    tie-off hackle-thorax
    whip-finish
}
